From 293f3dcaead535253b96a8c8d3634d3c2aee7456 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrew Dunstan <andrew@dunslane.net>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 11:00:02 -0400
Subject: [PATCH v13 1/2] Add amoptions callback to table access methods

Table AMs have had no way to define their own storage parameters:
CREATE TABLE and ALTER TABLE always parsed them with the heap parser,
whatever the table's access method.  Index AMs have long been able to
do this with IndexAmRoutine.amoptions; give table AMs the same.

TableAmRoutine gets an optional amoptions callback, with the same
signature as the index AM one.  table_reloptions() calls it if it's
set, and heap_reloptions() otherwise.  The bytea it returns is stored
in rd_options, in whatever layout the AM chooses.

Core code that reads StdRdOptions fields from rd_options, such as
RelationGetFillFactor() and vacuum_rel(), now checks
RelationHasStdRdOptions() first.  That is true if the AM has no
amoptions callback, or if it sets the new has_std_options_prefix flag
to say that its options struct begins with a StdRdOptions.  The
answer is worked out when the relcache entry's options are parsed
and kept in the new rd_stdoptions field, so the check costs no more
than the old test of rd_options.

add_reloption_to_kind() makes an existing option valid for another
relopt_kind, so that an AM can accept standard options without
defining them again.  This is also the only way for an AM to accept
autovacuum_* options that affect autovacuum, which always uses the
heap parser to read them.  add_std_reloptions_to_kind() does this
for all the standard options at once, and copies their parse table
entries for an AM whose options struct begins with a StdRdOptions.

ALTER TABLE ... SET ACCESS METHOD checks the table's options against
the new AM after applying any SET or RESET in the same command, and
raises an error if the new AM doesn't accept them.

src/test/modules/dummy_table_am provides two test AMs: dummy_table_am,
whose options struct begins with StdRdOptions, and
dummy_custom_table_am, whose options struct doesn't.

Discussion: https://www.postgresql.org/message-id/flat/ea1c4d33-0780-473c-96dc-1468cf733a04@dunslane.net
